126	
127	The `Policy` production is in PT8, in its own file type. The `Workflow` production is in PT10.
128	
129	- **PT2.1** Keywords are **contextual**: a keyword is reserved only in the grammar positions that use it, and anywhere an identifier is expected it is an ordinary identifier. So a field may be named `state`, `derived`, `internal`, `terminal` or `type`. The parser decides with at most two tokens of lookahead: after a field's initial expression, `derived` or `internal` followed by `:` begins the next field, and otherwise it is the modifier. Field and action names lower verbatim into `contract.json`, traces and diagnostics, so a corpus's own names are kept. There is no second, external name for a field. Identifiers follow TypeScript identifier rules. Rejection reasons and effect kinds are string literals, so agents can reuse the names already present in a system.
130	- **PT2.2** The expression sublanguage (AD-2) is a total, side-effect-free subset of TypeScript expressions:
131	  - literals, field and parameter names, `pre.` and `post.` (in step properties), enum members;
132	  - **name resolution (S19).** A bare name resolves to the innermost binding: a local (`let` or a pattern binder), then a parameter, then a field, as in TypeScript. `pre.f` always names the pre-state field `f`, in handlers as in properties, so a field hidden by a parameter of the same name stays readable. Parameters are visible only where the action is known: in a handler, and in a guard, step property or reject property only where `action == A` narrows it (PT5). With no narrowing, no parameter is in scope and a bare name is the field;
133	  - `== != < <= > >= && || !`, `+ - *`, and integer `/` and `%`. Division truncates toward zero, and `%` takes the sign of the dividend, as in TypeScript with the quotient truncated (`Math.trunc(a / b)`). A divisor whose static range contains 0 is `PL1204 DIVISION_BY_ZERO`, so division is total;
134	  - `implies`;
135	  - `emits(k, f: v, …)` (in step properties, PS5.2, S178): true iff the step emits effect `k` with those payload values. `k` is an effect's name, never a send, a timer or a spawn (`PL1001`); each `f` is a payload field of `k` (`PL1001`) and each `v` a constant of its type, an enum constructor, an integer, a boolean or `none` (`PL1201`);
136	  - `count(kind)` and `before(k1, k2)` (in path properties). Their arguments are effect kinds, string literals. `before("k1", "k2")` holds on a path iff every emission of `k2` is preceded by an emission of `k1` (S37);
137	  - `in { … }`;
138	  - in configuration properties only: `forall i in g: …`, `exists i in g: …`, the aggregates `count`, `sum`, `all` and `any` over `g[*]`, and `init(e)` for the initial value of `e` (PS13.6). The same quantifiers and aggregates range over a child family `f` in a compose invariant (S49);
139	  - map indexing `m[k]`, where `k` is a value of the map's key type;
140	  - map literals `{ k1: v1, …, kn: vn }`, which must list every key of the map's key type exactly once (`PL1502 INCOMPLETE_MAP_LITERAL`), and, for maps keyed by an integer range, the array literal `[v0, v1, …, vn]`; map update is the statement form `m' = m with [k] = v`, which writes one entry and leaves the others unchanged.
141	
142	  It has no loops, no calls to host functions and no string operations beyond equality and membership. Every expression terminates by construction. Precedence and associativity are fixed in PS12; the formatter parenthesises any mix of `implies` with `&&` or `||`.
143	  Primed fields are write-only: an expression never reads `f'` (PS2.3), and a field written twice on one path is `PL2102 DOUBLE_WRITE` (PS2.4).
144	- **PT2.3** Policies use PX, Polyaxion's three-valued (Kleene) language. The two logics are separated by **type**, not by syntax (PS8.0):
145	  - machine expressions have type `Bool`; policy inputs are optional, so policy expressions have type `Tri`;
146	  - one operator set (`==`, `!=`, `&&`, `||`, `!`, `in`) serves both, with its meaning given by the operand types;
147	  - `unknown(x)` has type `Bool` and is legal anywhere an optional value is in scope;
148	  - a `Tri` where a `Bool` is expected is `PL1203 TRI_WHERE_BOOL_EXPECTED`.
149	
150	  This follows the pattern of an option type, which agents know from TypeScript's `boolean | undefined`.
151	- **PT2.4** `e is C` tests a union value's constructor and has type `Bool`; `e is C1 | C2` is short for `e is C1 || e is C2`. `C { q: e }` constructs a union value. `match` is exhaustive (`PL1501 NON_EXHAUSTIVE_MATCH`). Enums are unions without payloads, so `==` and `is` agree on them.
152	  - **Names in patterns (S21).** In a payload pattern `C { q: x }`, the name `x` is read by these rules, in order: (1) a constructor of the payload's type is a literal pattern; (2) a constructor of another type in scope is `PL1201 TYPE_MISMATCH`, not a binder; (3) any other name binds the payload value. A binder that shadows a constructor in scope is an error (`PL1002`). A binder its arm never reads is `PL1504 UNUSED_BINDER`: write `_` to ignore a payload. An arm that the arms before it always cover is `PL1503 UNREACHABLE_ARM`. So a misspelled constructor cannot become a silent catch-all.
153	  - **Constructors shared by two types (S22).** A constructor name resolves against the expected type. In `a == b` (and `!=`), each side is typed first, and an ambiguous constructor on either side takes the other side's type, so `Open == s` and `s == Open` mean the same. The qualified form `Type.C` is always legal, in expressions and in patterns. A constructor still ambiguous after that is `PL1005 AMBIGUOUS_CONSTRUCTOR`, whose fix is the qualified form.
154	  - **Constructors and blocks.** In the condition of an `if` and the scrutinee of a `match`, an identifier followed by `{` is never read as a constructor: the `{` opens the block. Parentheses restore a constructor there, as in `if intent == (Proposed { decision: 1 }) { … }`. This is the rule Rust uses for the same ambiguity.
155	- **PT2.4c** **Claims at run time (PD5.1.2c, S155).** The claims travel with the action's data under the reserved key `$claims`, which no program may name and no outside dispatch may supply (`PL1208`). The lowered module's first acceptor applies PS3.2's claims row: claims missing, malformed, with a field outside its domain or with a field too many give `rejected("claims-unverified")`, before any guard. In check, a principal ranges over the representatives `p1` … `pn` of PS4.1c, which the contract's `dataDomain` lists; translation validation compares the row on missing, malformed and out-of-domain claims in every reachable state, and the deliberately broken lowering `skip-claims` leaves it out. At run time the host gives a verifier adapter at load (`load(…, { verifier })`, PD5.1.4): `rt.dispatch(id, A, d, { credentials })` asks it for the caller's claims, and an adapter that refuses or throws gives none, so the step is `rejected("claims-unverified")`, journaled, the state unchanged (PD5-A1, outage O10). poly checks the adapter's output against the type, never its cryptography (K11). Without one, the runtime uses the person-key adapter over the certified keyrings (S107): its credentials are a signed record of kind `invocation` whose subject is the digest of the one dispatch (instance, action, data, action id), and its output is `{ kind: Person, attest: Verified, assurance: 3, principal: <key owner> }`; an agent key, an unknown key or a record for another dispatch gives none (PD5-A8). PD4.2a is this adapter: `Declare` is written `by op: Caller` and requires a verified person (PD4-A3).
156	- **PT2.4d** **Lineage at run time (PD5.2, S105, S155).** The runtime writes each step's lineage in its transaction, beside the journal row: its origin, its intent and the digest of the previous hop, where a hop's digest binds the run, the seq, the origin, the intent and the hop before it. An outside dispatch starts a chain: its origin is its claims (or none), its intent its own machine and action. A step started by an effect's completion, by a fired timer or by a delivered message inherits the lineage of the step that caused it, found by the completion's, timer's or message's id, and appends one hop; an outside dispatch never inherits, whatever its action id. Lineage is runtime metadata, like ids and `seq`: the module never reads it, and the step event records its digest. A cross-scope message whose sending step's chain is missing or does not recompute is denied before the gate sees it, and `SendDenied(to, action, "lineage")` reaches the sender (PD5.2.2b). M5b limit: a child created in a cascade starts its own chain. **In check (S105).** Lineage is its origin class: a configuration's input with claims starts a chain whose class is its claims and its intent, `module.Machine.Action`; each message the step sends, each child it creates and each `SendDenied` the gate returns carries that class, and a delivered message's step inherits it. No hop is kept, so the global state stays finite: a message carries its class, and two messages that differ only in class are two (PD5-A5). A chain that starts with no claims has no class, so a program without claims is explored exactly as before. M5b limit: a completion or a timer given as a configuration's declared input (S61) is explored as an input, so a send from its step carries no class, where at run time it inherits one; no verdict reads the class, so only the counts differ (review C5).
157	- **PT2.4e** **Revocation (PD5.3, S106, M5b).** A security signal is an action on a mode machine, `security.Trust` in `conformance/m5/trust.poly`: `CredentialRevoked` and `AttestationFailed` take it to `Halted`, and only `Restore() by op: Caller` from a verified person at assurance 3 takes it back (PD5.3.3c, through the person-key adapter, PD5-A8). The halt is checked three ways. A policy's `never` statement that no compensable or irreversible effect is allowed while `machine("security.Trust").level == Halted` is proved over the decision table, and is a finding with a witness without the halt rule (PD5-A6, S149). The possibility property is the consistency stage per mode: a mandate whose only way to finish needs an irreversible effect is `PL6501` naming `Halted` (PD5-A7, PD4.4b). And no allow outlives the halt: an intent allowed before the revocation is rechecked when its handler would start, since the `Trust` singleton it read has moved, and the halt rule denies it (S147, PD5-A12).
158	- **PT2.4f** **Evidence beside claims (PD6, PS8.8, PS8.9, M9).** A verifier adapter may return its claims with evidence beside them, `withEvidence(claims, { verifiedAt?, statusAt?, mandates? })` (`@poly/runtime`): the times in milliseconds by the runtime's clock, and the mandate chain, outermost first, each `{ id, delegator, delegate, scope: { actions?, values?, max? }, expires }`. The evidence never enters `$claims`, whose type is the program's: the runtime keeps it with the step (`poly_evidence`), binds its digest into the step's ledger event as `evidence`, and `poly journal verify` checks the binding. A chain that widens, or holds a mandate revoked with `rt.revokeMandate(id)`, is refused before any step (`mandate-widened`, `mandate-revoked`, with an audit row). The gate reads `step.age`, `step.statusAge` and `step.mandate` from the evidence at the decision, and the worker denies `outside-mandate` before any policy when the innermost mandate does not cover the effect's kind, its payload or the decision time. A policy writes the ages as `age(step.claims)` and `statusAge(step.claims)`, in milliseconds. A rule over an age has severity `deny` (`PL1201` otherwise), and the gate reports its denial as `stale-claims`; the runtime never refreshes claims, and the host dispatches the action again with a fresh presentation (PS8.8, as ruled).
159	- **PT2.4b** **Claims (PD5.1.2, M5b).** `on A(x) by c: C { … }` names the claims type `C` the action requires and binds its value to `c` in the handler, as a parameter is bound. `C` must be a claims type (`PL1201` otherwise). The claims are part of the step's label (PS3.2, S101). On the embedded surface it is `on("A", ($) => […], ["x"], { by: ["c", "C"] })`, and a claims type is `claims("C", { … })`.
160	- **PT2.4a** **Handler parameters.** In `on A(x, y) { … }` the parameter list is optional. When present, it must repeat the action's declared parameter names in declaration order, otherwise it is `PL1201 TYPE_MISMATCH`. It neither renames nor selects parameters. The action's parameters are in scope in the body whether or not the list is written.
161	
162	- **PT2.5** **The embedded surface.** poly also has a surface embedded in TypeScript. It is valid TypeScript, type-checked by `tsc` against the combinators' declarations, and it produces **the same AST** as the standalone syntax, so the same AST digest (PT1.4). Every AST node has exactly one embedded form, and the mapping is syntactic: `poly-embed` never infers a type to decide which node a form is (S71).
163	
164	  ```ts
165	  import { module, enumType, machine, field, t, action, on, require, set, emit, effect, labels, cls, pathProperty, count } from '@cognitive-fab/poly/embed'
166	
167	  export default module('orders', [
168	    enumType('Status', ['Draft', 'Placed', 'Dispatched', 'Delivered', 'Cancelled']),
169	    machine('Order', {
170	      state: { status: field(t.named('Status'), ($) => $.Draft), items: field(t.range(0, 3), () => 0) },
171	      items: [
172	        action('Place', {}),
173	        action('Ship', { amount: t.int.check([10, 20]) }),
174	        on('Place', ($) => [require($.status === $.Draft, 'not-draft'), require($.items > 0, 'empty-order'), set('status', $.Placed)]),
175	        on('Ship', ($) => [require($.status === $.Placed, 'not-placed'), emit('dispatch', { amount: $.amount })]),
176	        effect('dispatch', { amount: t.int }, [labels(['egress:external']), cls('irreversible')]),
177	        pathProperty('dispatch-at-most-once', () => count('shipping.dispatch') <= 1),
178	      ],
179	    }),
180	  ])
181	  ```
182	
183	  | AST node | Embedded form |
184	  |---|---|
185	  | module and its declarations, in order | `export default module('m', [decl, …])` |
186	  | `enum E { A, B }` | `enumType('E', ['A', 'B'])` |
187	  | `type U = C1 { p: T } \| C2` | `union('U', { C1: { p: T }, C2: {} })` |
188	  | `type R = { f: T }` | `record('R', { f: T })` |
189	  | `use a.b` | `use('a.b')` |
190	  | `[service] machine M { version n  state { … } items }` | `machine('M', { service?: true, version?: n, state: { f: field(T, init, opts?) }, items: [item, …] })` |
191	  | field `f : T check D mode = e flag` | `f: field(T, ($) => e, { check?: D, mode?: 'strict' \| 'disclosed', flag?: 'internal' \| 'derived' })` |
192	  | types | `t.bool`, `t.int`, `t.string`, `t.range(lo, hi)`, `t.named('N')`, `t.map(K, V)`, `t.ref('M')`, `t.conf`, `t.int.check([…])` or `.check(lo, hi)` for a parameter's check clause |
193	  | `action A(p: T)` | `action('A', { p: T })` |
194	  | `on A [(x, …)] { body }` | `on('A', ($) => [stmt, …], ['x', …]?)`, the list only when written |
195	  | `guard all when e reject "r"` | `guard(($) => e, 'r')` |
