806	    action Propose()   action GateAllow()   action GateDeny()   action GateEscalate()
807	    action Approve()   action EscalationTimeout()   action Recheck()
808	    action Start()     action HandlerOk()   action HandlerFailed()   action HandlerFailedPermanent()
809	    action SettleOk()  action SettleFailed()   action WindowLapsed()
810	
811	    on Propose {
812	      match intent {
813	        Pending => intent' = Proposed { decision: 1, attempts: 0, rechecks: 0 }
814	        Escalated { approved: true, attempts: n, rechecks: r } => intent' = Proposed { decision: 2, attempts: n, rechecks: r }
815	        _ => reject "not-proposable"
816	      }
817	    }
818	    on GateAllow    { match intent {
819	                        Proposed { attempts: n, rechecks: r } => intent' = Allowed { attempts: n, rechecks: r }
820	                        _                                     => reject "no-proposal" } }
821	    on GateDeny     { require intent is Proposed else reject "no-proposal"
822	                      intent' = Denied }
823	    on GateEscalate { match intent {
824	                        Proposed { decision: 1, attempts: n, rechecks: r } => intent' = Escalated { approved: false, attempts: n, rechecks: r }
825	                        _                                                  => reject "escalate-once" } }
826	    on Approve      { match intent {
827	                        Escalated { approved: false, attempts: n, rechecks: r } => intent' = Escalated { approved: true, attempts: n, rechecks: r }
828	                        _                                                       => reject "not-waiting" } }
829	    on EscalationTimeout { match intent {
830	                        Escalated { approved: false, attempts: _, rechecks: _ } => intent' = Denied
831	                        _                                                       => reject "not-waiting" } }
832	    on Recheck      { match intent {
833	                        Allowed { attempts: n, rechecks: r } => if r < 2 { intent' = Proposed { decision: 1, attempts: n, rechecks: r + 1 } }
834	                                                                else     { intent' = Denied }
835	                        _                                    => reject "not-allowed" } }
836	    on Start        { match intent {
837	                        Allowed { attempts: n, rechecks: r } => intent' = Running { attempt: n + 1, rechecks: r }
838	                        _                                    => reject "not-allowed" } }
839	    on HandlerOk    { match intent {
840	                        Running { attempt: n, rechecks: r } => intent' = Settled { attempt: n, rechecks: r }
841	                        _                                   => reject "not-running" } }
842	    on HandlerFailed { match intent {
843	                        Running { attempt: n, rechecks: r } => if n < 3 { intent' = Allowed { attempts: n, rechecks: r } }
844	                                                               else     { intent' = Dead }
845	                        _                                   => reject "not-running" } }
846	    on HandlerFailedPermanent { match intent {
847	                        Running => intent' = Dead
848	                        _       => reject "not-running" } }
849	    on SettleOk     { require intent is Settled else reject "not-settled"
850	                      intent' = Done }
851	    on SettleFailed { match intent {
852	                        Settled { attempt: n, rechecks: r } => if n < 3 { intent' = Allowed { attempts: n, rechecks: r } }
853	                                                               else     { intent' = Dead }
854	                        _                                   => reject "not-settled" } }
855	    on WindowLapsed { match intent {
856	                        Allowed => intent' = Dead
857	                        _       => reject "not-allowed" } }
858	
859	    step property no-reproposal-after-allow:
860	      (action != Recheck && (pre.intent is Allowed || pre.intent is Running || pre.intent is Settled))
861	        implies !(post.intent is Proposed)
862	    liveness settles: eventually intent is Done | Denied | Dead
863	      under fair { Propose, GateAllow | GateDeny | GateEscalate, EscalationTimeout, Start,
864	                   HandlerOk | HandlerFailed | HandlerFailedPermanent, SettleOk | SettleFailed }
865	  }
866	  ```
867	
868	  The state has 17 representable values, and all 17 are reachable. The v0.3 encoding as five fields had 432 representable combinations, of which 33 were reachable, plus three invariants and two step properties whose job was to rule out the rest. With the union, those properties are types:
869	
870	  - a second gate decision after execution cannot be written, because `Allowed`, `Running` and `Settled` carry no decision;
871	  - a deny after execution cannot be written, because `GateDeny` only matches `Proposed`;
872	  - `executed` is not a field, because it is `intent is Running || intent is Settled || …`.
873	
874	  One step property is kept as a regression guard, and the liveness property holds with `HandlerFailedPermanent` in the fair group of the handler's outcomes (S138), which adds three transitions and no state. **A stale allow is rechecked (S147).** `Recheck` takes `Allowed` back to `Proposed`, keeping the attempts made, when a singleton the allow read has moved on; each intent is rechecked at most twice, and a third stale allow is `Denied` with reason `stale-allow`. The bound keeps `settles` true under weak fairness, since `Recheck` is not in a fair group. The step property becomes "no re-proposal except by `Recheck`". With the rechecks the machine has 17m + 8 states at bound m: 59 at the bound 3, all reachable. An independent exploration of the machine before S138 ([`reviews/evidence/outbox_sum_check.py`](reviews/evidence/outbox_sum_check.py)) confirms 17 reachable states, no deadlock and no fair cycle outside the terminal states.
875	
876	  Three rules follow from the machine and are normative:
877	
878	  1. **One decision per execution.** Once the gate allows an intent, a retry after a timeout or a failed settle goes back to `Allowed`, not to `Proposed`. The gate is not asked again for the same idempotency key, except by `Recheck`, when the state the allow read has moved on (S147). In v0.4 this is a consequence of the type.
879	  2. **Escalation happens once and has a timeout.** An approved re-proposal can be allowed or denied, never escalated again, so an intent takes at most two gate decisions. `escalation.timeoutMs` (the polyflow platform policy field) fires `EscalationTimeout`, which denies the intent.
880	  3. **Denial is final.** A deny can only happen from `Proposed`: before any attempt ran, or after a `Recheck` found the state the allow read had moved on (S147), when the attempts already made are kept and no further one is.
881	  4. **A permanent failure is final (S138).** A handler may report a permanent error (a declined card is a result, not an infrastructure failure). `HandlerFailedPermanent` takes `Running` to `Dead` whatever attempt it is, the worker dispatches the effect's `failed` completion, and no further attempt is made. A crash is never permanent: it is `HandlerFailed`.
882	  5. **A lapsed key window ends the intent (PT33.5a, S120).** For a keyed effect, once the window has lapsed since the first attempt began, the worker takes `WindowLapsed`, which takes `Allowed` to `Dead` and starts no further attempt. It adds a transition and no state, so the machine still has 17m + 8 states at bound m, and it is not in the fair group (the worker's clock takes it, as it takes `Recheck`). The worker delivers the effect's `exhausted` completion with the observation outcome `lapsed`.
883	
884	  **The worker hosts the machine (S119).** poly-runtime runs its own effect worker, and polyrun's `tickEffects` is never started. Each intent's state is an `OutboxIntent` value in a poly-runtime table, and every worker transition is an action on the lowered `OutboxIntent` module, written in one transaction with the matching `pr_outbox` change. An action the module rejects is a runtime fault: the worker stops on that intent and reports it, and never takes the transition. A terminal transition and the completion it delivers commit in one transaction, behind the claim's fence: K1 extends to the hosted machine, so neither exists without the other, and a worker whose claim was taken over delivers nothing (review C1, C2). `Approve` and `EscalationTimeout` race, and whichever commits first wins; the other is a rejected step of the machine, journaled with its reason, and a late approval's caller gets an error saying the escalation timed out (review C3). Every park has a retry deadline. An outside dispatch to a hosted instance is refused before any step, with an audit row (review C4). A gate that throws holds only the intent it was asked about, `Proposed` with a backoff and never allowed (review C6, PT9.5). An intent keeps the bound it was created under: the runtime registers the machine of every bound the build declares and of every bound an intent in the store was created under, so a redeploy that changes a manifest's `maxAttempts` strands nothing (review C8). The machine for bound m is `runtime<m>.OutboxIntent` (`runtime.OutboxIntent` at 3), and these ids are the runtime's own: a program machine in a module named `runtime` or `runtime<m>` is `PL1208`, and `load` refuses one again, so whether a program loads never depends on the bounds or the store (S143). The only module the checker takes under those names is this model at a bound, identified by its module digest. A crash or lease expiry while `Running` is `HandlerFailed` and counts an attempt; the idempotency key (PT15) keeps a re-run of an effect that did execute from repeating it. A row the worker cannot place (machine or manifest unavailable) takes no transition. `Dead` is terminal: an operator who wants another try creates a new intent through a governed action, never by resurrecting a dead one.
885	
886	  **The attempt bound (S120).** The machine above is the instance with `maxAttempts` 3. The runtime's CI checks it once per distinct `retry.maxAttempts` in the build, with `Allowed`, `Running` and `Settled` ranged over that bound, and the worker conformance runs at each. `load` refuses a manifest entry without `maxAttempts`, and admission refuses one above 10 (PT19, S137). The runtime's CI checks every bound from 1 to 10 and records the state count of each.
887	
888	  **Each handler outcome is observed (S138).** With every `HandlerOk`, `HandlerFailed` and `HandlerFailedPermanent`, the worker writes an `observation` event on the emitting run, in the same transaction, naming the intent, the attempt and the outcome the handler gave (`ok`, `failed`, `permanent`, or `lost` for a crash). The runtime's CI pairs each observation with the transition taken, so a worker that retries a permanent error is `PL2401`.
889	
890	  The check of this machine is gate M4b in PT18. Adding a state or a transition to the worker without changing this machine first is `PL2401 RUNTIME_MODEL_DRIFT` in the runtime's own CI.
891	
892	- **PT9.8 The delivery protocol is a checked machine.** "Each message takes exactly one step at its receiver" (PS14.3) depends on the outbox worker, the network path and the receiver's dedupe table working together, with retries and archiving in between. The protocol is specified as a poly machine, with the network's choices as the environment's inputs, and checked. Two messages from one sender to one receiver are enough to exhibit every interleaving that matters: loss, duplication by resend, acknowledgement loss, archiving and ordering.
893	
894	  ```poly
895	  module delivery
896	
897	  type Msg = M1 | M2
898	  type Status = Unsent | Pending | InFlight | Acked
899	
900	  service machine Delivery {
901	    state {
902	      status   : map<Msg, Status> = { M1: Unsent, M2: Unsent }
903	      req      : map<Msg, 0..2>   = { M1: 0, M2: 0 }            // request copies in the network
904	      ack      : map<Msg, 0..2>   = { M1: 0, M2: 0 }            // acknowledgement copies in the network
905	      lost     : map<Msg, 0..1>   = { M1: 0, M2: 0 }            // the network loses at most one copy per message
906	      seen     : map<Msg, bool>   = { M1: false, M2: false }    // the receiver's dedupe table (K4)
907	      steps    : map<Msg, 0..2>   = { M1: 0, M2: 0 }            // steps taken at the receiver
908	      archived : bool = false
909	    }
910	    action Emit(m: Msg)     action Send(m: Msg)       action Resend(m: Msg)
911	    action LoseReq(m: Msg)  action DeliverReq(m: Msg) action LoseAck(m: Msg)
912	    action DeliverAck(m: Msg)  action Archive()
913	
914	    on Emit(m) {
915	      require status[m] == Unsent else reject "emitted"
916	      require m == M1 || status[M1] != Unsent else reject "emit-order"
917	      status' = status with [m] = Pending
918	    }
919	    on Send(m) {
920	      require status[m] == Pending && req[m] < 2 else reject "not-pending"
921	      require m == M1 || status[M1] == Acked else reject "in-order"      // the in-order worker
922	      status' = status with [m] = InFlight
923	      req' = req with [m] = req[m] + 1
924	    }
925	    on Resend(m) {
926	      require status[m] == InFlight && req[m] < 2 else reject "not-in-flight"
927	      req' = req with [m] = req[m] + 1
928	    }
929	    on LoseReq(m) {
930	      require req[m] > 0 && lost[m] < 1 else reject "no-loss"
931	      req' = req with [m] = req[m] - 1
932	      lost' = lost with [m] = 1
933	    }
934	    on DeliverReq(m) {
935	      require req[m] > 0 else reject "nothing-in-transit"
936	      req' = req with [m] = req[m] - 1
937	      if ack[m] < 2 { ack' = ack with [m] = ack[m] + 1 }
938	      if !seen[m] { seen' = seen with [m] = true
939	                    steps' = steps with [m] = steps[m] + 1 }
940	    }
941	    on LoseAck(m) {
942	      require ack[m] > 0 && lost[m] < 1 else reject "no-loss"
943	      ack' = ack with [m] = ack[m] - 1
944	      lost' = lost with [m] = 1
945	    }
946	    on DeliverAck(m) {
947	      require ack[m] > 0 else reject "no-ack"
948	      ack' = ack with [m] = ack[m] - 1
949	      if status[m] == InFlight { status' = status with [m] = Acked }
950	    }
951	    on Archive {
952	      require !archived && steps[M1] == 1 && steps[M2] == 1 else reject "not-done"
953	      archived' = true                                  // the dedupe table is kept (K4)
954	    }
955	
956	    invariant at-most-once: steps[M1] <= 1 && steps[M2] <= 1
957	    invariant in-order: steps[M2] >= 1 implies steps[M1] >= 1      // M2 never steps before M1 (S64)
958	    liveness exactly-once:
959	      eventually (status[M1] == Acked && status[M2] == Acked && steps[M1] == 1 && steps[M2] == 1)
960	      under fair { Emit(*), Send(*), Resend(*), DeliverReq(*), DeliverAck(*) }
961	  }
962	  ```
963	
964	  Losses are not in the fairness list: the network may lose, but only finitely often (`lost` bounds it). The results of an independent exploration ([`reviews/evidence/delivery_check.py`](reviews/evidence/delivery_check.py)) are:
965	
966	  | Variant | Result |
967	  |---|---|
968	  | In-order worker, dedupe kept on archive (the machine above) | 1,147 states; `at-most-once` and `in-order` hold; `exactly-once` holds under the stated fairness |
969	  | Parallel worker (no `in-order` guard), dedupe kept | 2,368 states; `at-most-once` and `exactly-once` hold |
970	  | Parallel worker, `in-order` required | violated in 4 steps: `Emit(M1), Emit(M2), Send(M2), DeliverReq(M2)` |
971	  | In-order worker, dedupe cleared on archive | `at-most-once` violated in 10 steps: `M1` is resent, delivered, acknowledged; `M2` is delivered; the receiver is archived; the duplicate of `M1` arrives and takes a second step |
972	
973	  Three rules follow and are normative for the runtime:
974	
975	  1. **Dedupe records live as long as the instance** (K4). Archiving keeps them.
976	  2. **`fifo` requires the in-order worker.** A pair declared `fifo` is served by a worker that sends a message only after the previous one to the same receiver is acknowledged. For co-located pairs this is local, in-order dispatch.
977	  3. **Unordered delivery is the default.** Without `fifo`, the parallel worker is used. Exactly-once still holds, and order is not promised.
978	
979	  The runtime's conformance to this machine is kernel assumption K8, checked by trace replay at M4. polyrun's message worker is the parallel one, so its traces replay against the parallel variant (`examples/delivery/delivery-parallel.poly`), through an abstraction function written down in the test: `Emit` is the sender's commit, `Send` a claim, `Resend` a claim after a lease recovery, `LoseReq` a crash before the receiver commits, `DeliverReq` the receiver's commit or dedupe hit, `LoseAck` a crash between that and the mark, `DeliverAck` the mark `done`, `Archive` the purge (S125).
980	- **PT9.9 Sends across trust scopes.** A `send` from one trust scope to another is proposed to the gate like an effect (PS8.7). The proposal carries `kind: "send"`, the target's machine and scope, the action, and the payload digest. On deny, the runtime does not deliver the message. It delivers `SendDenied(to, action, reason)` to the sender instead, with its own idempotency key. Sends within one scope are not proposed. **As built (M5a, S160).** An instance's trust scope is its machine's module, unless its configuration assigns one with `scope "x"`. A policy reads a send through `proposal.kind == "send"` and `send.to` (the target machine), `send.action` and `send.scope`. The runtime proposes each message between two scopes in the sender's session before polyrun delivers it: allowed, it is delivered; denied, it is marked dead and `SendDenied(to, action, reason)` is dispatched to the sender with the action id `<message>:send-denied`; a gate that cannot decide leaves it pending. A message has one gate decision: a delivery retried after an allow goes under the recorded allow, and a recorded deny is finished without a new proposal. The proposal's `send` also carries the target instance (`send.instance`) and the payload's digest (`send.payloadDigest`), so the verdict binds the data it allowed. A send has no approval path in M5a, so an escalation of one is denied, with the reason saying so. The configuration check explores the gate's choice on every message between instances of different scopes: delivered (allow), turned into `SendDenied` to the sender (deny), or held and then delivered or denied (escalate). **M5a limit:** a scope is a module and a cross-module address is not lowered (`use`, PL0901), and the runtime has no configuration instances before S153 (E5), so no program makes a cross-scope send at runtime in M5a; the runtime path is tested with a message injected below the language, and M5b adds the end-to-end test.
981	
982	## PT10. Workflows
983	
984	```poly
985	workflow customer-brief runs Brief {
986	  input Start
987	  key "{date}" where date matches "^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$"
988	  tool "brief.post" -> slack_send target "#cs" why "publish the approved brief"
989	}

